[TEST] Circular wait: observe five held first forks, then stop every demonstration worker
[ENV] Java 21.0.10; test caller=main
[CONCEPT] Deadlock comes from a resource cycle, not merely from a slow or timed-out program.
[SETUP] Pi acquires Fi, then waits for F(i+1)%5. A latch ensures all five first forks are held.
[RECOVERY] The demonstration interrupts all workers before joining any; each worker releases acquired forks in finally.
[OBSERVED] Snapshot[heldFirstForks=5, workersStopped=true]
[ASSERT] held first forks: expected=5; observed=5
[WHY] The coordinated ownership snapshot establishes the cycle; no second fork can be obtained.
[ASSERT] all demonstration workers stopped: expected=true; observed=true
[WHY] A teaching deadlock must recover and leave no demonstration worker alive after returning.
[PASS] Circular wait: observe five held first forks, then stop every demonstration worker — all assertions completed successfully.

